WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.138
When several teams build a system at the same
time, agreeing on API behavior allows them to

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:10.333
make progress before every implementation is
finished. A messaging client can initially call

00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:15.359
a mock inbox service that returns a fixed list
of messages in the agreed response format.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.567
In this example, notice the same client first
connected to a mock and later to the real

00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:25.750
service through the same contract. This helps
developers build parsing, presentation, and

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:30.875
basic error handling early. It does not prove
end-to-end correctness: a mock that always

00:00:30.875 --> 00:00:35.767
returns the same inbox will not reflect newly
sent messages, enforce permissions, or

00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:38.795
reproduce real persistence and concurrency
behavior.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.623
The same backend contract can serve several
kinds of client. A mobile application, a desktop

00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:50.247
application, and a browser interface may all
fetch messages, send messages, and authenticate

00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:54.647
through the same service API while presenting
different user interfaces.

00:00:54.647 --> 00:01:00.140
In a traditional web design, a server builds
HTML; in a single-page design, browser code

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:05.508
obtains data and updates the interface. In this
example, notice different presentation

00:01:05.508 --> 00:01:11.250
layers converging on the same domain operation.
These choices form a continuum, and a system

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.995
can combine server-rendered pages with browser
interactions.

00:01:15.147 --> 00:01:20.263
Moving presentation to the browser does not move
the authority to approve operations there:

00:01:20.263 --> 00:01:22.849
the backend must still validate every request.
